Tolerate KVM_SET_TSC_KHZ failing with EINVAL on restore.

Without hardware TSC scaling (Intel client / Coffee Lake Xeon E parts,
anything before Skylake-SP), KVM accepts a guest TSC rate above the host rate
(catch-up mode) but rejects one below it with EINVAL and logs
"kvm: user requested TSC rate below hardware speed". Cloud Hypervisor turned
that into a hard restore failure, so a live migration from a 3.3 GHz node to a
3.4 GHz node always aborted at the State step while the reverse direction
worked. QEMU warns and continues in this situation; do the same. Guests on
kvm-clock or the Hyper-V reference TSC page keep correct time because the
host recomputes the pvclock scale from the real TSC rate.

--- a/hypervisor/src/kvm/mod.rs
+++ b/hypervisor/src/kvm/mod.rs
@@ -2838,7 +2838,13 @@
     fn set_tsc_khz(&self, freq: u32) -> cpu::Result<()> {
         match self.fd.set_tsc_khz(freq) {
             Err(e) => {
-                if e.errno() == libc::EIO {
+                if e.errno() == libc::EINVAL {
+                    warn!(
+                        "KVM refused guest TSC rate {freq} kHz (no TSC scaling on this host); \
+                         the guest TSC keeps the host rate"
+                    );
+                    Ok(())
+                } else if e.errno() == libc::EIO {
                     Ok(())
                 } else {
